            <td data-label="What it tests">{item.tests}</td>
            <td data-label="Real pass-rate reality">{item.passRate}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Pass-rate figures are drawn from published exam-result tracking across recent sittings and
    genuinely fluctuate paper to paper and sitting to sitting. Treat the ranges above as a picture of
    real volatility, not a fixed promise for your specific attempt. Verify current session-by-session
    results against the Institute's own published results before making a study-timeline decision.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Why this matters more here than in most careers: a candidate who clears Core Principles
      confidently, even with strong marks, still faces a separate, genuinely hard filter at Core
      Practices, and then another one at Specialist Advanced. Clearing one stage tells you almost
      nothing about your odds at the next one, because each stage tests a different kind of thinking,
      recall and technique early on, applied judgement and specialist depth later. Plan your timeline
      and your morale around passing through several distinct gates, not around getting past "the
      exam" once.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="timeline">How long Fellowship actually takes</h2>

  <p>
    The Institute of Actuaries of India's own guidance is that "depending on the efforts put in, one
    may get through the examinations in 4 to 6 years on average," alongside a separate minimum
    3-year work-based-skills requirement that generally runs in parallel with your exam years rather
    than strictly after them.
  </p>

  <p>
    The more realistic number, and the one that matches how the pass rates above actually behave, is
    closer to <strong>7-10 years</strong> from your first exam to Fellowship for someone working
    full-time while studying. One qualified actuary's own account, cited alongside India's shortage
    figures, put their journey at 2015 to September 2024, nine years, while working full-time
    throughout.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Do not plan your finances, your marriage timeline, or your own sense of self-worth around the
      "4-6 years on average" figure. Treat it as a best-case number for someone with an unusually
      light workload, strong prior maths training, and no failed sittings along the way. A realistic
      plan budgets for 7-10 years, multiple repeat attempts on at least one or two papers, and treats
      those repeats as normal, not as evidence you are not cut out for this.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ifoa-vs-iai">IAI or IFoA: which body to sit exams with</h2>

  <p>
    Indian students choose between two exam-granting bodies: the Institute of Actuaries of India
    (IAI) and the UK's Institute and Faculty of Actuaries (IFoA). The choice affects cost and
    exemptions more than final career outcomes.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">IAI (India)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Significantly cheaper per paper: a subject like CS1 runs roughly Rs 11,500, against roughly Rs 22,000-34,000 for the same subject through IFoA.</li>
        <li>Offers subject exemptions for chartered accountants, cost accountants, company secretaries, and graduates from institutes like the IITs, IIMs, and ISI, which can reduce your total paper count.</li>
        <li>An IAI Fellowship converts into an IFoA Fellowship later by paying a conversion fee, with no additional exams, so starting here does not close off international recognition.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">IFoA (UK)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Carries a stronger global brand for candidates who are certain, from the start, that they will build a career outside India.</li>
        <li>Costs meaningfully more per paper, an important factor across a pipeline you will likely sit for years and multiple attempts.</li>
        <li>International employers have grown more cautious about some recent IFoA exam formats amid concerns over unproctored online testing, worth checking current guidance on before committing.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    For most students planning a career centred in India, or open to either, starting with IAI and
    keeping the IFoA conversion option open later is the financially lower-risk default. The exception
    is a student who already knows, with real certainty, that they are building toward a UK, European,
    or other IFoA-recognised market career from day one.
  </p>

