  <h2 id="ceiling">How to actually raise your income ceiling</h2>

  <p>
    A fair salary table only tells you where most people land, not where the strongest outcomes
    actually come from. This field's income ceiling is not fixed by Fellowship alone; it is set by how
    far past "qualified actuary" a person is willing and able to specialise and move.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      The clearest ceiling-raising moves inside this field are: choosing a genuine specialisation,
      reinsurance treaty pricing, health reserving, or pensions, where genuinely few candidates are
      competent, moving from a domestic insurer into a global reinsurer's offshore analytics hub once
      real technical depth exists, pursuing the Appointed Actuary route deliberately once Fellowship
      and years of relevant experience are in place, and pairing actuarial technique with AI-assisted
      modelling fluency so you are the person interpreting and governing the models, not the person
      whose routine calculations they replaced.
    </p>
    <p>
      None of these are guaranteed outcomes, and none happen from clearing exams alone. They happen
      for candidates who pair technical depth with visible on-the-job proof and enough communication
      skill to make their findings usable to people who are not actuaries, not just correct on paper.
    </p>
  </div>

